--TEST--
Twenty values through a `Core\Task\Channel` of capacity one arrive exactly once each and in the order they were sent, counted rather than read off a line — `rule:concurrency/nothing-is-still-running-when-a-call-returns`'s "nothing still running" over `nvs_stdlib::channel`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A capacity of one makes every send past the first wait, so this sweep is
// nineteen suspensions of the producer and nineteen of the consumer. What it
// asserts is a property of the whole run rather than any one line of it: a
// channel that dropped a value, delivered one twice, or handed them back out
// of order fails here while still looking right on a three-element case.
class Tally {
    public static int $count = 0;
    public static int $sum = 0;
    public static int $last = 0;
    public static bool $ordered = true;
}

var $chan = new Core\Task\Channel<int>(1);

Core\Task::all({
    produce: fn(): int => {
        foreach (Core\Arr::range(1, 20) as int $n) {
            $chan->send($n);
        }
        $chan->close();
        return 20;
    },
    consume: fn(): int => {
        foreach ($chan as int $n) {
            if ($n != Tally::$last + 1) {
                Tally::$ordered = false;
            }
            Tally::$last = $n;
            Tally::$count += 1;
            Tally::$sum += $n;
        }
        return Tally::$count;
    },
});

// 20 values and 1+2+…+20 = 210. A dropped value moves both; a duplicate moves
// both the other way.
echo Tally::$count, " ", Tally::$sum, "\n";

if (Tally::$ordered) {
    echo "in order", "\n";
} else {
    echo "reordered", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
20 210
in order
